# Question: if the data we store grows by a few percent every month, what
# will storage cost in two years, what will the two years cost in total, and
# how likely is the monthly bill to exceed 3k dollars by then?
#
# Run it (it uses the shared formulas):
#   ubc --scenario cloud/storage-growth
# Change an assumption, for example a retention policy that deletes old data:
#   ubc --set 'growth = 1% to 3%' --scenario cloud/storage-growth
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens bill_later --scenario cloud/storage-growth
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Amounts are in dollars.
# Growth compounds: 5% a month is not 120% in two years but 223%, so a
# small change in the monthly rate moves the result a lot.
#
# Sources: public price lists of the cloud providers, for storage prices; the
# data volume and growth come from your own metrics.

# Assumptions
data_tb = 20 to 50                # terabytes stored today; from your storage metrics
growth = 3% to 8%                 # how much the data grows every month; typical experience, check your history
price_per_tb = 20 to 25           # dollars per terabyte a month for your storage class; check the provider's price list
months = 24                       # horizon

# Calculation
data_later = compound(data_tb, growth, months)                       # terabytes stored at the end of the horizon
bill_later = data_later * price_per_tb                               # the monthly bill then
total = (data_later - data_tb) / growth * price_per_tb               # every month's bill added up (a geometric series)

# Result
data_later
bill_later
total
P(bill_later > 3k)                # how likely is storage to cost more than 3k dollars a month in two years?

# Output (seed 1, 5000 samples):
#   data_later = ≈ 101   90% [52.5 … 223]   98% [41 … 350]   ▄▇█▇▅▄▃▂▂▂▁▁▁▁▁▁
#   bill_later = ≈ 2260   90% [1170 … 5060]   98% [900 … 7700]   ▄██▇▅▄▃▂▂▂▁▁▁▁▁▁
#   total = ≈ 31.8k   90% [18.3k … 58k]   98% [14k … 75k]   ▃▆▇██▇▆▄▃▃▂▂▂▁▁▁
#   P(bill_later > 3k) = 0.27
